No other copies. Let me search npm cache for luxon tarballs of newer versions.

Actually, maybe simpler: reason about the correct fix myself and make it robust, matching the "universally-accepted meaning of is in DST".

The universally-accepted meaning: a DateTime is in DST if its zone's offset at that instant is greater than the zone's standard offset (i.e., the offset is shifted forward from standard time).

The current implementation approximates standard offset by looking at Jan 1 and May 1 (well, same day-of-month in Jan and May) of the same year and taking the minimum-ish via `>` comparisons. The bug: in 1974 New York, DST ran from Jan 6 to Oct 27, so the January reference date (same day-of-month) may itself be in DST, making the comparison fail.

The minimal fix used upstream (I'm fairly confident): add `day: 1` to the January set:

```js
